# Angular and TypeScript Coding Rules

## TypeScript Formatting

### Braces and Indentation

- Opening braces must always be on a new line
- Use 4 spaces for indentation (never tabs)
- All control structures (if, for, while, etc.) must use braces, even for single-line blocks
- Class declarations must have opening brace on a new line
- Method declarations must have opening brace on a new line

### Decorators

- Decorators (e.g., `@Input()`, `@Output()`, `@Component()`) must be on their own line
- Each decorator must be on a separate line from the property/class it decorates

### Access Modifiers

- Always explicitly specify access modifiers (`public`, `private`, `protected`) on class members
- Properties and methods should use `public` when they need to be accessible outside the class

### Properties and Methods

- Add line breaks between logical groups of code for readability
- Keep each logical operation visually separate from others
- Optional properties must use the `?:` syntax
- Properties should be grouped logically (e.g., all `@Input()` properties together, then `@Output()` properties, then methods)
- Sort properties and attributes alphabetically within their groups

### Type Annotations

- Always provide explicit type annotations for method parameters and return types
- Use type aliases for commonly used types
- Prefer explicit types over `any`
- For finite string types (enums), always use algebraic string union types instead of plain `string`
- Example: `export type ButtonType = 'button' | 'submit' | 'reset';` instead of `string`

### Spacing

- Add blank lines between logical groups of code
- Add blank lines between imports and code
- Add blank lines between type definitions and classes

## HTML Template Formatting

- Sort all attributes alphabetically
- Format templates with one attribute per line for readability
- Keep `class` attribute first and event handlers (like `(click)`) last when sorting

## HTML Semantics and Layout

- Use semantic elements wherever they apply: `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<main>`, `<nav>`, `<section>`, `<article>`, `<aside>`, `<figure>`, `<figcaption>`, form elements (`<button>`, `<input>`, `<label>`, `<fieldset>`, `<legend>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>`, `<progress>`), and list structure (`<ul>`, `<ol>`, `<li>`) instead of generic `<div>`/`<span>` when the meaning fits
- Prefer Flexbox or CSS Grid for layout; avoid layout patterns that rely on floats or table display when flex/grid are appropriate

## Comments

- Use JSDoc comments for public APIs when documentation is needed
